            <ArticleTitle>Vitamin D and chronic rhinitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>13-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/ACI.0b013e32834eccdb</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE OF REVIEW" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To discuss the role of vitamin D in chronic rhinitis and chronic rhinosinusitis (CRS).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RECENT FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Vitamin D has been shown to have an immunomodulatory effect with a significant impact on immune function. Specifically, vitamin D regulates the mechanisms which suppress the inflammatory response and direct the differentiation fate of immune cells. Vitamin D has been shown to play an important role in asthma, and the concept of the unified airway model allows the extrapolation of vitamin D as a critical player in chronic rhinitis and rhinosinusitis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Recent findings on the function of vitamin D may explain aspects of the pathophysiology of chronic rhinitis and CRS, and may help direct future treatment of these diseases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
